+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface (amendment)
+
+**Branch**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-09 | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This amends [001's contracts/cli.md](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/cli.md). Every clause of that
+contract stays in force except where this document replaces it, and C-01…C-26 remain valid tests
+with one textual change, noted under **`--count` tally** below. New contract IDs continue at C-27.
+
+Like its predecessor, every clause here is observable from outside the process — arguments and files
+in; stdout bytes, stderr bytes, exit status and the contents and permissions of files in DIR out.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [--] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` and Python 3.11 or newer (A-10).
+
+### Argument grammar (allowlist — SR-016, replacing SR-001's table)
+
+| Token | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `--count` | Write a tally to stderr after all output. Unchanged, including that a repeat is accepted. |
+| `--out-dir DIR` | Write each FILE's redacted lines to `DIR/<basename of FILE>` instead of stdout. `--out-dir=DIR` is equivalent (A-21). At most once. |
+| `--` | End of options. Every later token is a file operand, even one starting with `-`. |
+| anything else not starting with `-` | A file operand. |
+
+The allowlist stays closed. Rejected, with a diagnostic on stderr, nothing on stdout, no directory
+created, and a non-zero status: `--out_dir`, `--out`, `--out-di` and any other abbreviation,
+`--out-dir` with no value, a second `--out-dir`, and everything 001 already rejected (`--cou`,
+`--count=1`, `-h`, `--help`, `--verbose`, …).
+
+> A second `--out-dir` is rejected rather than last-one-wins. FR-023 says "at most once", and
+> silently honouring one of two different destinations is the kind of silent wrong answer
+> Principle II forbids. See [research.md R18](../research.md).
+
+### Operands
+
+Unchanged from 001 — order honoured, repeats read again, opened read-only exactly as supplied, never
+written or truncated — with these additions when `--out-dir` is in effect:
+
+- At least one operand is **required**. `--out-dir` with no operand is a usage error: stdin is not
+  read, DIR is not created, stdout stays empty, status 2 (FR-031, A-16).
+- The output file for an operand is named by the operand's **final path component only**. Directory
+  parts of the operand are discarded and never recreated inside DIR (FR-024, SR-010).
+- An operand that cannot be opened or read is reported and skipped as before, and **no output file
+  is created for it**; the remaining operands are still processed (FR-028).
+- An operand whose output path names that same file — by path, hard link or symbolic link — is
+  refused before anything is truncated; the operand is left unmodified and the remaining operands
+  are still processed (FR-036, SR-012).
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Without `--out-dir` | With `--out-dir` |
+|---|---|---|
+| stdin | Read only when there are no operands | Never read (an operand is required) |
+| stdout | The redacted input, and nothing else | **Only** the single `wrote N files` line — no redacted line, ever (FR-024) |
+| stderr | Diagnostics and the `--count` tally only | Unchanged: diagnostics and the tally only (SR-014, SR-017) |
+
+Redacted lines written to a file in DIR carry the same byte-for-byte guarantees stdout carries:
+matches replaced by their placeholders, every other byte reproduced exactly, `\n`, `\r\n`, a lone
+`\r` and a missing final terminator preserved, invalid UTF-8 bytes reproduced unchanged (FR-009,
+A-4).
+
+## Output folder
+
+| Property | Behaviour | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Does not exist | Created, with every missing parent | FR-025 |
+| Exists as a directory | Used as it is; no file in it that no operand names is removed or altered | FR-025 |
+| Exists, not a directory | Diagnostic naming DIR; no operand read; stdout empty; status 2 | FR-029 |
+| Cannot be created | Diagnostic naming DIR; no operand read; stdout empty; status 2 | FR-029 |
+| Empty value (`--out-dir ""`) | Cannot be created, so the same failure | FR-029 |
+| Permissions of a directory the tool creates | Owner-accessible only, set at creation — asserted as `mode & 0o077 == 0` so it holds under any umask | SR-011 |
+
+## Output files
+
+| Property | Behaviour | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Path | `DIR` + the operand's final path component; nothing else contributes, and no input data does | FR-024, SR-010 |
+| Name already present in DIR | Content replaced in full, no remnant of the previous content | FR-026 |
+| Permissions of a file the tool creates | Owner-read/write only, set at creation | SR-011 |
+| Permissions of a file that already exists | Left exactly as its owner set them — neither widened nor narrowed | SR-011 |
+| Symbolic link at the output path | Write refused and reported, link not followed, target untouched and never created | SR-010 |
+| Output path is the operand being read | Write refused, operand unmodified, reported, remaining operands processed, status 2 | FR-036, SR-012 |
+| Cannot be opened, written, flushed or closed | Reported naming the output path, remaining operands processed, status 2; that operand not counted in N | FR-030 |
+| Writing | Line by line as the input is read; one line and one output handle in memory at a time | FR-034 |
+| Empty input file | An empty output file is created and counted | spec Edge Cases |
+
+## Exit status
+
+The three statuses of 001 are unchanged — `0`, `2`, `141` — and no status is added. A non-zero
+status always means the output is incomplete and must not be treated as a complete redaction, for
+files in DIR exactly as for stdout.
+
+### Whether the run continues after a failure (replacing 001's table)
+
+| Failure | Remaining operands | `wrote N files` written? | When the status is decided |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Operand cannot be opened or read | **processed**, one diagnostic each | **yes**, N excluding it | 2, at the end of the run |
+| Output file cannot be opened/written/flushed | **processed** | **yes**, N excluding it | 2, at the end of the run |
+| Output path is the operand | **processed** | **yes**, N excluding it | 2, at the end of the run |
+| Line exceeds the 1 MiB cap | **not** processed; the run stops | **no** (FR-033) | 2, at once |
+| DIR cannot be created or is not a directory | nothing is read | **no** | 2, before any operand is opened |
+| `--out-dir` with no operand | nothing is read | **no** | 2, before DIR is created |
+| Rejected argument | nothing is read or written | **no** | 2, before any I/O |
+| stdout write/flush failure | run ends | n/a | 2, at once |
+| stdout closed early | run ends | n/a | 141 |
+
+An output file already written stays written and complete. An operand whose read failed part way
+leaves the output file it had already opened, holding the lines read before the failure; it is not
+counted in N ([research.md R22](../research.md)). A-25: output is written in place, never staged
+through a temporary file, so a run stopped early can leave a short output file — the status and the
+absence of `wrote N files` are what tell the caller not to trust the folder.
+
+## Output formats
+
+### Placeholders
+
+| Kind | Replacement |
+|---|---|
+| email address | `[EMAIL]` |
+| IPv4 address | `[IP]` |
+| **phone number** | **`[PHONE]`** |
+| payment card number | `[CARD]` |
+
+Fixed literals, not configurable (A-23). A match is replaced in full, leaving no character and no
+length information from the original (SR-006). Recognition rules and precedence:
+[redaction-rules.md](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+### `--count` tally
+
+Exactly one line on stderr, written after all redacted output has been written and flushed (A-3):
+
+```
+emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N
+```
+
+The `phones` field is **appended** to 001's three, so the first three fields are unchanged for a
+caller that parses them positionally (A-15). Note that the field order is therefore *not* the
+precedence order. Every other property is unchanged from 001: `N` counts replacements made across
+all inputs in the run (per occurrence, not per distinct value, not per file); the line contains
+nothing else — no sample values, no line numbers, no file names (SR-014); it is written only when
+`--count` is given **and** the run exits 0; and without `--count` a successful run writes nothing at
+all to stderr.
+
+> **Effect on C-04, C-06, C-18, C-26 and the 001 test suite**: the expected tally text gains
+> ` phones=N`. That is the only change SC-012 permits in the existing suite, and the only existing
+> contract clause this amendment rewrites.
+
+### `wrote N files`
+
+Exactly one line on stdout, written only with `--out-dir`, after every operand has been processed
+and every output file has been closed, and before the `--count` tally:
+
+```
+wrote N files
+```
+
+`N` is the decimal number of **operand outputs written in full** during the run: the operand's input
+reached end of file with no read error and its output handle closed with no error. The format is
+fixed and never pluralised — one file reports `wrote 1 files` (FR-027). The line contains nothing
+but those literals and the count (SR-014).
+
+Consequences of "operand outputs written", from A-18: two operands sharing a final path component
+report `2` although one file remains, and the same operand named twice reports `2` although its
+redaction is written twice to one file.
+
+### Diagnostics
+
+One line on stderr per failure, prefixed with the program name, containing no input data and no
+traceback (SR-017). 001's four shapes are unchanged; these are added:
+
+| Situation | Shape | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| DIR cannot be created or is not a directory | `python -m logredact: error: cannot create output directory 'DIR': REASON` | FR-029 |
+| `--out-dir` with no FILE operand | `python -m logredact: error: --out-dir requires at least one FILE operand` | FR-031 |
+| `--out-dir` given more than once | `python -m logredact: error: --out-dir may be given at most once` | FR-023 |
+| Output path is the operand being read | `python -m logredact: error: refusing to write 'OUTPATH': it is the input file 'FILE'` | FR-036 |
+| Output file cannot be opened/written/flushed | `python -m logredact: error: cannot write 'OUTPATH': REASON` | FR-030 |
+
+`DIR` and `OUTPATH` are derived only from the caller's own arguments — DIR as written on the command
+line, and DIR plus an operand's final path component. `REASON` is the operating system's `strerror`
+(for example `File exists`, `Permission denied`, `No space left on device`,
+`Too many levels of symbolic links`). No message contains an input line, a matched value, or any
+fragment of one (SR-017).
+
+## Behavioural contract
+
+New cases. C-01…C-26 of 001 still apply, with the tally text amended as noted above.
+
+### Phone recognition (Story 1, FR-019…FR-021)
+
+| ID | Given (stdin, no options unless stated) | Then |
+|---|---|---|
+| C-27 | `call +14155552671 now\n` | stdout exactly `call [PHONE] now\n`; status 0 |
+| C-28 | `+442071838750\n` | stdout exactly `[PHONE]\n` |
+| C-29 | `+12345678\n` (8 digits) and `+123456789012345\n` (15 digits) | each line exactly `[PHONE]\n` — both bounds inclusive |
+| C-30 | `+1234567\n` (7 digits) | stdout exactly `+1234567\n` |
+| C-31 | `+1234567890123456\n` (16 digits) | no `[PHONE]` in stdout and no digit of the run removed by the phone rule |
+| C-32 | `14155552671\n` (no `+`) | stdout exactly `14155552671\n` |
+| C-33 | `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n` | stdout exactly `user [EMAIL] from [IP]\n` — existing categories untouched |
+| C-34 | `paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123\n` | stdout exactly `paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123\n` — card rule untouched |
+| C-35 | `+14155552671@example.com\n` | stdout exactly `[EMAIL]\n`, counted once as an email |
+| C-36 | `++14155552671\n` | stdout exactly `+[PHONE]\n` |
+| C-37 | `99+14155552671\n` | stdout exactly `99[PHONE]\n` (A-14) |
+| C-38 | each of `+abc`, `+`, `+ 14155552671`, `+1 415 555 2671`, `+1-415-555-2671` | unchanged by the phone rule |
+| C-39 | a phone number at the very start and at the very end of a line, with and without a terminator | redacted identically to one in the middle |
+| C-40 | `+4111111111111\n` (13 Luhn-valid digits) | stdout exactly `[PHONE]\n`, **not** `+[CARD]\n` (A-13) |
+| C-41 | `+41111111 11119\n` | stdout exactly `[PHONE] 11119\n` — the recorded consequence of FR-021 ([research.md R17](../research.md)) |
+
+### `--count` (Story 3, FR-022, SR-014)
+
+| ID | Given | Then |
+|---|---|---|
+| C-42 | `a@b.io +442071838750\n`, `--count` | stdout exactly `[EMAIL] [PHONE]\n`; stderr exactly `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1\n`; status 0 |
+| C-43 | input with no redactable values, `--count` | stderr exactly `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0 phones=0\n`; status 0 |
+| C-44 | `a@b.io 1.2.3.4\n`, `--count` | stderr exactly `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0 phones=0\n` |
+| C-45 | several operands, `--count` | one line of run totals including `phones`, not per file |
+| C-46 | `--count --out-dir clean` over readable operands | stderr exactly the four-field tally; stdout exactly `wrote N files\n`; no tally text in any output file |
+| C-47 | `--out-dir clean` without `--count`, successful run | stderr empty |
+
+### Output folder, success (Story 2, FR-023…FR-027, FR-034, SR-011)
+
+| ID | Given | Then |
+|---|---|---|
+| C-48 | `--out-dir clean app.log web.log`, `clean` absent | `clean` created; `clean/app.log` and `clean/web.log` hold the redaction of each input; stdout exactly `wrote 2 files\n`; stderr empty; status 0 |
+| C-49 | any successful `--out-dir` run | no redacted line on stdout; stdout is exactly the `wrote N files` line |
+| C-50 | `--out-dir clean logs/2026/app.log` | output is `clean/app.log`; no `logs` or `2026` directory inside `clean` |
+| C-51 | `--out-dir a/b/c`, none of `a`, `b`, `c` existing | all three created; output files appear in `a/b/c` |
+| C-52 | `clean/app.log` exists with longer unrelated content | it holds exactly the redaction of `app.log`, no remnant |
+| C-53 | `--out-dir clean app.log` | stdout exactly `wrote 1 files\n` |
+| C-54 | `--out-dir clean app.log`, `app.log` readable | `app.log` byte-for-byte unchanged, size and mtime unchanged |
+| C-55 | FILE operands and **no** `--out-dir` | exactly today's behaviour: every operand's redaction on stdout in operand order, no `wrote N files` line |
+| C-56 | `clean` exists holding `unrelated.txt` | `unrelated.txt` is still there, unchanged |
+| C-57 | an empty input file with `--out-dir` | an empty output file of that name exists and is counted |
+| C-58 | `--out-dir clean a/app.log b/app.log` | one `clean/app.log` holding the redaction of `b/app.log`; stdout exactly `wrote 2 files\n` (A-18) |
+| C-59 | `--out-dir clean app.log app.log` | `clean/app.log` holds one redaction of it; stdout exactly `wrote 2 files\n` (A-18) |
+| C-60 | any directory or file the tool creates | `mode & 0o077 == 0` at the moment it exists (SC-020) |
+| C-61 | `clean/app.log` pre-created mode `0o644` | after the run it is still `0o644` and holds the redaction (SR-011) |
+
+### Output folder, failure (Story 4, FR-028…FR-033, FR-036, SR-010, SR-012, SR-016, SR-017)
+
+| ID | Given | Then |
+|---|---|---|
+| C-62 | `--out-dir clean app.log missing.log web.log` | `clean/app.log` and `clean/web.log` complete; no `clean/missing.log`; stderr names `missing.log`; stdout exactly `wrote 2 files\n`; status 2 |
+| C-63 | `--out-dir` naming an existing regular file | stderr names that path; no operand read; no output file; stdout empty; status 2 |
+| C-64 | `--out-dir` under a parent that denies write permission | stderr names that path; stdout empty; status 2 *(skipped as root — [R25](../research.md))* |
+| C-65 | `--out-dir clean` with no FILE operand | stderr describes the rejected usage; stdin not read; stdout empty; `clean` not created; status 2 |
+| C-66 | `--out-dir clean app.log b.log` where `clean/app.log` cannot be opened for writing | stderr names the output path; `b.log` still processed; status 2 *(skipped as root)* |
+| C-67 | `--out-dir . app.log b.log`, run in `app.log`'s directory | `app.log` not truncated or modified; stderr names it; `b.log` still processed; status 2 |
+| C-68 | `--out-dir clean` and an operand whose second line is 1 MiB + 1 characters | run stops; no part of the over-length line in any output file; stderr names the input source and reports the length; **no** `wrote N files` line; status 2 |
+| C-69 | `--out_dir clean a.log`, `--out clean a.log`, `--out-di clean a.log` | stderr describes the rejected option; stdout empty; no directory created; status non-zero |
+| C-70 | `--out-dir` as the last argument with no value | stderr describes the rejected usage; status non-zero |
+| C-71 | any failing `--out-dir` run | stderr has no Python traceback and no contents of any input file |
+| C-72 | `--out-dir "" app.log` | stderr names the empty DIR; stdout empty; status 2 |
+| C-73 | `clean/app.log` is a symlink to a file outside `clean` | write refused; stderr names the output path; the target's bytes unchanged; status 2 |
+| C-74 | `clean/app.log` is a **dangling** symlink to a path outside `clean` | write refused; that path is **not** created; status 2 |
+| C-75 | `--out-dir a --out-dir b app.log` | stderr describes the rejected usage; neither `a` nor `b` created; status 2 |
+
+C-27, C-42 and C-48 are the spec's three worked examples (SC-011).
