+# Contract: redaction rules (amendment)
+
+**Branch**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-09 | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This amends [001's contracts/redaction-rules.md](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/redaction-rules.md).
+The email, IPv4 and card rules, the Luhn check, the candidate scan and every pass-through guarantee
+are **unchanged**. What changes: a fourth alternative, and a four-way precedence order.
+
+Validated in a prototype against the cases listed at the end ([research.md R15–R17](../research.md)).
+
+## Precedence and scanning
+
+One compiled pattern, now **four** named alternatives in precedence order (FR-021, amending FR-014):
+
+```
+(?P<email>…) | (?P<ip>…) | (?P<phone>…) | (?P<card>…)
+```
+
+Still applied once per line with `pattern.sub(replacer, line)`, so the required semantics come from
+`re` itself and not from bookkeeping: leftmost match wins, matches never overlap, alternatives are
+tried in written order at each position, and replaced text is never re-scanned (FR-021). Each call
+of the replacer is one counted replacement (A-1).
+
+How each new relation is actually decided — this is a property of the design a reviewer should know,
+established in [research.md R16](../research.md):
+
+| Relation | Decided by | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| email over phone | the written alternation order; both can start at the same `+` | `+14155552671@example.com` → `[EMAIL]`, counted once as an email |
+| phone over card | the leftmost rule; a phone match starts at `+`, a card run at a digit, so they can never start at the same position | `+4111111111111` → `[PHONE]`, not `+[CARD]` (A-13) |
+| phone vs IPv4 | cannot overlap at all: a phone match holds only `+` and digits, an IPv4 match needs three dots | ordering is for symmetry only (A-13) |
+
+## Phone number → `[PHONE]`
+
+```
+\+[0-9]{8,15}(?![0-9])
+```
+
+| Element | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `\+` | FR-019: a literal `+` is required and is part of the match, so it is replaced with the digits |
+| `[0-9]` not `\d` | FR-019 says *decimal* digits and A-4 limits the rules to ASCII; `\d` also matches non-ASCII decimal digits |
+| `{8,15}` | FR-019's inclusive bounds: 8 digits match, 15 match, 7 and 16 do not |
+| `(?![0-9])` | FR-020: a candidate must not be followed by a digit, so a 16-or-more-digit run contains no phone number and no leading part of it is replaced |
+| no lookbehind | A-14 deliberately places no constraint on what precedes the `+` |
+
+**Matches** (each replaced in full by `[PHONE]`, with nothing of the original left):
+
+| Input | Output | Note |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+14155552671` | `[PHONE]` | |
+| `+12345678` | `[PHONE]` | 8 digits, lower bound |
+| `+123456789012345` | `[PHONE]` | 15 digits, upper bound |
+| `++14155552671` | `+[PHONE]` | the match starts at the second `+` |
+| `99+14155552671` | `99[PHONE]` | digits before the `+` are not part of a phone number (A-14) |
+| `+4111111111111` | `[PHONE]` | also a Luhn-valid card candidate; phone wins (A-13) |
+| at the start or end of a line, with or without a terminator | `[PHONE]` | no positional constraint |
+
+**Does not match**:
+
+| Input | Output | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+1234567` | unchanged | 7 digits, below the bound |
+| `+1234567890123456` | unchanged by the phone rule | 16 digits: the lookahead rejects every length (FR-020) |
+| `14155552671` | unchanged | no `+` |
+| `+abc`, `+`, `+ 14155552671` | unchanged | `+` not immediately followed by a digit |
+| `+1 415 555 2671`, `+1-415-555-2671` | unchanged by the phone rule | separators inside the run; the digit groups are then considered by the card rule as they are today |
+| `+14155552671@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | the email rule has higher precedence (FR-021) |
+
+**Cost**: at most 8 attempts at any start position (lengths 15 down to 8 over one character class),
+so the work per position is constant and the rule is linear in line length — measured linear from
+48 KiB to 1 MiB on five adversarial shapes, worst case 425 ms for a full 1 MiB line (SR-015,
+[research.md R15](../research.md)). Peak memory is unchanged: one line, no match list (FR-034).
+
+## Interaction with the card rule
+
+The one place existing output changes, recorded because it is a deliberate choice rather than an
+accident ([research.md R17](../research.md)):
+
+| Input | 001 | After this amendment | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `+4111111111111` (13 Luhn-valid digits) | `+[CARD]` | `[PHONE]` | intended — A-13 |
+| `+4111111111111111` (16 digits) | `+[CARD]` | `+[CARD]` | unchanged — the phone rule cannot fire |
+| `+12345678 4111 1111 1111 1111` | `+12345678 [CARD]` | `[PHONE] [CARD]` | both values still removed |
+| `+41111111 11119` | `+[CARD]` | `[PHONE] 11119` | accepted consequence of FR-021's no-re-scan clause |
+
+The last row is the only narrowing of redaction: a card candidate whose first digit group is 8–15
+digits long loses that group to the phone rule, and the remaining groups are too short to form a
+candidate. Real card groupings (4-4-4-4, or 4-6-5 for Amex) never have an 8–15 digit first group,
+and an unseparated card after a `+` is unaffected (row 2). No digit of the phone number survives,
+which is what SR-006 requires of the winning kind. Pinned by C-41.
+
+## Pass-through guarantees
+
+Unchanged from 001, and now also for a file in DIR: every byte that is not part of a match is
+reproduced exactly — text, separators between adjacent matches, line terminators (`\n`, `\r\n`, a
+lone `\r`, a missing final one) and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 (FR-008, FR-009, A-4). The output
+file uses the same stream settings as stdout, so there is one set of guarantees, not two
+([research.md R20](../research.md)).
+
+## Prototype validation
+
+`python3 -I /tmp/proto/phone.py` — 18 of 18 cases drawn from Story 1, Story 3 scenario 1 and the
+spec's Edge Cases pass, plus the digit-bound, 16-digit, A-13 and overlap tables above.
+
+`python3 -I /tmp/proto/overlap.py` —
+
+- a 48-line corpus of genuine international numbers (8–15 digits; at the start, middle and end of a
+  line; beside an email) is 100% redacted with no four-digit fragment of any of them surviving
+  (SC-013);
+- an 11-line near-miss corpus (`+` with 7, 16 and 20 digits; no `+`; `+` then a space or a letter;
+  separator-grouped; bare `+`; `++`) produces 0 phone redactions (SC-014);
+- an exhaustive search over first-group lengths 8–15 and total lengths 13, 14 and 19 found the 38
+  inputs of the `+41111111 11119` shape and no other change to existing card behaviour.
